-- title:
--   Central extensions of a dicyclic pair meet the commutator trivially
-- statement:
--   Let $G$ be a group, let $a,b \in G$ and let $t$ be a natural number such that $b a b^{-1} = a^{-1}$, $b^{2} = a^{t}$ and $a$ has order exactly $2t$. Let $E$ be a group and let $\pi \colon E \to \langle a, b\rangle$ be a group homomorphism onto the subgroup `Subgroup.closure {a, b}` of $G$ generated by $a$ and $b$, assumed surjective, whose kernel is contained in the centre of $E$, so that $\pi$ presents $E$ as a central extension of $\langle a,b\rangle$. The conclusion is that $\ker \pi \sqcap \mathrm{commutator}\, E = \bot$, i.e. the intersection of $\ker \pi$ with the derived subgroup $[E,E]$ is the trivial subgroup of $E$. Note that the dicyclic relations are imposed on the elements $a, b$ of the ambient group $G$, the order condition $\mathrm{orderOf}\, a = 2t$ being part of the data; no finiteness or further hypothesis on $G$ or $E$ is required.
--
--   This is the stem-triviality of dicyclic (for $t$ a power of $2$, generalised quaternion) groups — the vanishing of their Schur multiplier — stated in the form ‘kernel meets derived subgroup trivially’ for an arbitrary central extension of a dicyclic generator pair inside an ambient group. It is used by [`Ihara.exists_pow_prime_pow_eq_one_of_sl2_stem`](thm.html#Ihara.exists_pow_prime_pow_eq_one_of_sl2_stem), where it is applied to the normalisers of split and nonsplit tori in $\mathrm{SL}_2$ over a finite ring.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_Ihara_ker_inf_commutator_eq_bot_of_dicyclic_closure_pair.lean

